        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "6647ee46-b5ea-5b83-9e67-23f0f650d084",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Paul Hindemith, Gioachino Rossini, Andrea Leone Tottola, Sir Walter Scott, Maria de Francesca-Cavazza, Wolfgang Sawallisch, Francesco Tortoli\n- Works: Cardillac, La donna del lago, The Lady of the Lake, La gazzetta, Otello, Armida, Mosè in Egitto\n- Organizations: Deutsche Grammophon\n- Topics: opera, libretto, set design, world premieres\n\nFacts:\n- In 1810, Sir Walter Scott wrote the narrative poem The Lady of the Lake.\n- La donna del lago is an opera composed by Gioachino Rossini with a libretto by Andrea Leone Tottola based on the French translation of Sir Walter Scott's 1810 poem The Lady of the Lake.\n- Francesco Tortoli created sets for numerous productions, including the world premieres of Gioachino Rossini's La gazzetta, Otello, Armida, Mosè in Egitto, and La donna del lago.\n- Cardillac is an opera by Paul Hindemith in three acts and four scenes.\n- In 1985, Maria de Francesca-Cavazza appeared as Cardillac's daughter in Paul Hindemith's opera Cardillac on a Munich DVD conducted by Wolfgang Sawallisch and issued by Deutsche Grammophon.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "130b2635-a332-5b11-a253-50e5eb0ebe5f",
        "text": "Page: Cardillac. Sentence: Cardillac is an opera by Paul Hindemith in three acts and four scenes.\nPage: La donna del lago. Sentence: La donna del lago (The Lady of the Lake) is an opera composed by Gioachino Rossini with a libretto by Andrea Leone Tottola (whose verses are described as \"limpid\" by one critic) based on the French translation of \"The Lady of the Lake\", a narrative poem written in 1810 by Sir Walter Scott, whose work continued to popularize the image of the romantic highlands.\nPage: Maria de Francesca-Cavazza. Sentence: She can be heard and seen in the role of the Cardillac's daughter in Hindemith's opera Cardillac, conducted by Wolfgang Sawallisch, on a 1985 Munich DVD issued by Deutsche Grammophon.\nPage: Francesco Tortoli. Sentence: He was the creator of sets for numerous productions including those for the world premieres of Rossini's \"La gazzetta\", \"Otello\", \"Armida\", \"Mosè in Egitto\", and \"La donna del lago\".",
